WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.362
This slide focuses on choosing only the columns we
need The query

00:00:04.388 --> 00:00:08.850
selects MRN full name and age from the patients
table It does

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:12.775
not return patient ID gender or active status
because those fields are

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.325
not needed for this particular question The ORDER BY
clause sorts age

00:00:17.338 --> 00:00:21.925
in descending order DESC means the largest value
comes first so the

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:25.938
oldest patients should appear at the top of the
result Using an

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:30.988
explicit column list has several benefits First it
makes the purpose of

00:00:31.012 --> 00:00:35.675
the query easier to understand When someone reads
the SQL they can

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:41.038
immediately see what the output should contain
Second it avoids retrieving columns

00:00:41.062 --> 00:00:45.700
that are not needed Third it makes downstream code
more predictable because

00:00:45.725 --> 00:00:49.650
the result has a clear set of fields SELECT star can
still

00:00:49.662 --> 00:00:52.250
be useful when you are exploring a small table and
want to

00:00:52.275 --> 00:00:56.725
quickly see all columns But it should be a
deliberate choice not

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:01.250
an automatic habit Run the query and inspect the
order of ages

00:01:02.138 --> 00:01:07.225
Then try changing DESC to ASC ASC means ascending
order so the

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.288
youngest patient should move toward the top This
small change is a

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.175
good way to see how ORDER BY shapes the result
without changing

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.162
the table itself
